== Impact from stats ==

Necromancers are amazing in that they are pretty good even with low stats. A lot of their power is hard coded into their spells. The need for huge mana pools is less important for Necromancers than for Wizards, Mages or Clerics who do not have this advantage. After reading, you can decide for yourself, and unlike some classes, even dumping all your points into a poor stat will not break your Necromancer, as they are very self-sufficient.

* '''Strength'''     - Main benefit is carrying more weight. Weight reduction bags solve this problem and no points are recommended to be spent here.
* '''Stamina'''      - Affects your maximum [[Game_Mechanics#Hitpoint_Calculation|hit points]]. Necromancers get 2 hitpoints per point of Stamina at 50 and 2.4 at 60. Stamina is beneficial for Necromancers because they frequently go up and down in health in combat even if they are not being hit due to Lich and lifetaps.
* '''Dexterity'''    - The rate of raising weapon skills and weapon procs (effects) going off. Necromancers are not a melee class, and beyond around level 8 you will almost entirely give it up.
* '''Agility'''      - Agility adds to your avoidance. There is a movement penalty below 75 AGI, so you will want to put 5 points in this if you plan to roll an Erudite.
* '''Wisdom'''       - Affects the learning of general and craft skills if higher than Intelligence, but does not improve necromancer spell casting.
* '''Intelligence''' - The main caster stat for Necromancers. Adds to your maximum mana; at 60, it adds about 11 mana per point of INT, up to the soft cap of 200 INT, after which it adds 5. Intelligence affects the speed at which you learn spell skills, craft skills and general skills. This is a great stat, both for power and for convenience. Your skills will raise faster and you can cast more spells before having to meditate. Necromancers have incredibly fast mana regen, but more maximum mana will benefit you.
* '''Charisma'''     - Will affect resists on undead only lulls beginning with Numb the Dead. Also affects vendor prices. It does not affect other parts of the necromancer kit.

====Health vs. Mana====
Due to the mechanics of Lich, passively reducing the Necromancer's health every tick in exchange for a boost in mana per tick, Necromancers have, in effect, two pools of mana; the mana they directly draw from spells, and their health pool acting as a mana battery, constantly being drained to provide more mana for the Necromancer's use. So how much health and how much mana should you have?

We should begin by understanding that each of these have a certain advantage. A sizeable mana pool means that when you are coming out of an extended rest (meaning that you will be at full mana and health), you will be able to cast more spells in a small period of time. A sizable health pool means that while you are fighting and engaged in combat, the negative effects of Lich will not be as pressing, and you will have more wiggle room in which to use Lifetaps to restore your health.

A great deal of this comes down to personal taste and your own playstyle. Following is a series of considerations that one should keep in mind when finding their "sweet spot" for a mana/health ratio. Just remember that both health and mana are effectively power to fuel your spells, but that each of them works differently: Mana is a readily available, immediately exploitable resource to cast your spells, while Health is not immediately available, but makes your life much easier and safer.

===== Tips and Notes =====

Obviously, when your HP is full (or very close) you want to focus on gaining Mana; Lifetapping would be wasteful. The same is true in reverse: Lich is wasteful when your Mana is full.

When neither is full, your priorities should depend on two things: your "squishiness" (relative to the mob), and your pace.  If you are facing tougher foes (ie., feeling squishy) you'll want to keep your HP higher, as mana won't help if the mob gets some lucky hits in and kills you. At the same time, you'll also want to take into account whether you are facing a steady stream of individual mobs, or bursts of several. If you can't rely on having enough time for your Lich spell to provide more mana in-between fights, you may want to focus on keeping your mana higher in-between fights.

However, all of this boils down to one simple goal. Never be full health, never be full mana.

Regarding Charisma, it should be noted that your lull spells (such as [[Numb the Dead]] and [[Rest the Dead]]) use [[Statistics#Charisma_.28CHA.29|Charisma]] as part of their formulas, but most would agree it is not worth gearing for. This does not mean that it is worthless to do so, but it is worth considering if you are running into problems using these spells.

As an aside; some may claim that the Necromancer's [[Screaming Terror]] Mesmerize or Charm spell lines are affected by Charisma. 

== Religion ==

Religion is not a huge concern to Necromancers as they are already hated.  As a necromancer primary your core choices will be Cazic-Thule, Innoruuk, or Bertoxxulous. If you take necromancer as a secondary class, your deity is instead restricted by your primary class choice. This allows you to get around some of the faction hits of being a necromancer, but be careful that you don't end up reverse KoS to necromancer trainers and spell vendors (see: any paladin)!

Your race determines which deity you can pick; humans can notably choose between Innoruuk and Bertoxxulous, which allows you to start in Freeport or Qeynos respectively as a necromancer primary.

